 ## Overview
-Dynamic Cache Depth Scheduling is a heuristic strategy that adjusts the depth of reuse for cached computation results (specifically anchor-to-Gaussian derivations) in subsequent frames based on the intensity of decoding calculations in the current frame [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of maintaining rendering quality and suppressing rapid appearance changes caused by sudden cache flushing in large-scale Gaussian Splatting models [S1, unofficial]. Unlike static cache reuse, this method dynamically balances performance and visual stability by modulating how many previous frames' results are retained [S1, unofficial].
