 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Stale Appearance:** Maintaining the same Gaussian appearance parameters in the cache for too long causes significant rendering errors, particularly on trajectories around a centered target [S1, unofficial]. This is handled by flushing the cache in time via the max reuse depth limit [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rapid Appearance Changes:** Static cache reuse depth can cause rapid changes in scene appearance when the cache is flushed. Dynamic scheduling suppresses this by gradually adjusting the depth [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Rapid Appearance Changes:** Static cache reuse depth can cause rapid changes in scene appearance when the cache is flushed [S1, unofficial]. Dynamic scheduling suppresses this by gradually adjusting the depth [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the anchor decoding stage and Gaussian rasterization stage. By reusing cached Gaussians, the decoding stage is skipped for duplicate anchors, reducing computational load [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the cache depth $D_{max}$ and the number of anchors. With $D_{max}=10$, the cache stores results from up to 10 previous frames [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Tested on Scaffold-GS and OctreeGS models. Setting $D_{max}=10$ balances performance and quality [S1, unofficial]. It is effective for both single GPU and multi-GPU resources [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Maintains image rendering quality without significant decrease or fluctuation; suppresses rapid appearance changes caused by sudden cache flushing; model-agnostic [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the guiding function $H$ and duplicate rate calculation; may introduce latency if cache management is inefficient [G].
